mdb_strerror on Windows: keep the system's message in a thread's buffer

Why: on Windows mdb_strerror formats the text of a system error into a
buffer on its own stack and returns the buffer's address (the "HACK"
comment: the caller is to read it before anything overwrites it). The
address is not valid in the caller, and MinGW-w64's gcc at -O2 (the
upstream Makefile's flags, which the tests use) warns
(-Wreturn-local-addr) and returns a null pointer instead: the MinGW-w64
build's tools print "(null)" for the text of every system error, and a
program that reads the text crashes (KNOWN_C_BUGS.md row 75). The
interop tests compare C 1.0's tool messages with rlmdb's, which carry
the system's text, on both Windows toolchains.

What: the buffer is a thread-local static array of the same size; the
4 KiB pad is gone. The line count of the file is unchanged, so line
numbers cited elsewhere stay valid.

Upstream: not reported; not fixed upstream at the pinned commit.

diff --git a/mdb.c b/mdb.c
index 54e15a15..6cf577cb 100644
--- a/mdb.c
+++ b/mdb.c
@@ -1942,13 +1942,13 @@ char *
 mdb_strerror(int err)
 {
 #ifdef _WIN32
-	/** HACK: pad 4KB on stack over the buf. Return system msgs in buf.
-	 *	This works as long as no function between the call to mdb_strerror
-	 *	and the actual use of the message uses more than 4K of stack.
-	 */
+	/* rlmdb's patch 0002: system messages in a buffer of the thread */
 #define MSGSIZE	1024
-#define PADSIZE	4096
-	char buf[MSGSIZE+PADSIZE], *ptr = buf;
+#ifdef _MSC_VER
+	static __declspec(thread) char buf[MSGSIZE]; char *ptr = buf;
+#else
+	static __thread char buf[MSGSIZE]; char *ptr = buf;
+#endif
 #endif
 	int i;
 	if (!err)
